    "field": "Ocean biogeochemistry",
    "problem": "The extent to which the slowdown of the Atlantic meridional overturning circulation contributed to the observed reduction in carbon dioxide uptake in the subpolar North Atlantic has remained uncertain, with numerical models showing discrepancies and lacking sufficient observational constraints.",
    "method": "The authors use observational data on the transport of volume, heat, and carbon dioxide from repeated ocean transects to construct regional carbon budgets, separating anthropogenic from natural components to link a reduction in CO\u2082 uptake to the weakening of the meridional overturning circulation.",
    "task": "The task is to use oceanic transport data to determine why carbon dioxide uptake in the subpolar North Atlantic declined, attributing the reduction to a weakening meridional overturning circulation that primarily affected the natural, rather than anthropogenic, carbon component.",
